oll_sd": 21.72, "poll_weight": 0.09, "fundamentals": {"margin": 14.88, "lean": 2.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 2.58}, "rating_margin": 20.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.25, "baseline_margin": 17.44, "analyst_adjustment": -2.44, "key_factors": ["Pat Ryan's massive financial advantage: $4.7M raised and $3.1M on hand versus under $90K for Auringer", "Prediction markets price Ryan at roughly 93% on both Kalshi and Polymarket", "A July Democratic-sponsored poll showed Ryan ahead 53-39 among likely voters", "Modest D-leaning district (PVI D+2) with a favorable 2026 environment for Democrats"], "rationale": "Every signal points the same way: a large cash edge, a double-digit poll lead, and markets pricing Ryan near 93%. The fundamentals baseline of roughly +15 fits comfortably, with only the ethics complaint about his DC residence and a thin polling record warranting slight caution.", "watch": "The DC-residence ethics complaint gaining traction or a late outside-spending surge for Auringer could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Newest usable poll is 66 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Pat Ryan (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Democrat Pat Ryan seeks reelection in a Hudson Valley district that leans slightly Democratic. He enters the general election with an overwhelming resource advantage, having raised about $4.7 million and holding $3.1 million on hand, while Republican Jacqueline Auringer has raised under $90,000. A July poll of likely voters showed Ryan ahead 53 to 39, and both major prediction markets price his win probability around 93 percent.\n\nThe campaign has turned negative, with both candidates criticizing attacks as beyond the pale, and Ryan faces an ethics complaint over a Washington-area residence. Still, no independent expert ratings flag the race as competitive, and the combination of money, po